= Introduction

The quantitative determination of protein is among the most frequent operations
of the biochemical laboratory, yet the methods in common use leave much to be
desired. The biuret reaction @wu1922, in which the peptide bonds of protein form
a violet complex with cupric ion in alkali, is convenient and remarkably free of
interference, but it is insensitive: a milligram or more of protein is needed for
a reliable reading. Direct measurement of ultraviolet absorption near 280 m$mu$
is rapid and consumes no sample, but it is thrown off by nucleic acid and by any
absorbing impurity, and it asks for protein in the same order of quantity.

The Folin--Ciocalteu phenol reagent @folin1927, on the other hand, is reduced by
the tyrosine and tryptophan of protein to give an intense blue, and is sensitive
to small amounts; but the colour depends on the aromatic-residue content, which
varies from protein to protein, and the reagent is reduced only slowly by protein
alone. Herriott observed that prior treatment with alkaline copper greatly
enhances the colour subsequently given with the phenol reagent @herriott1941.

The method described here exploits that enhancement. Protein is first allowed to
react with copper in alkali, as in the biuret reaction; the phenol reagent is
then added. The chromogen so produced is far more intense than either reaction
alone would yield, and the determination becomes sensitive to a few micrograms of
protein while retaining much of the convenience of the biuret method.
